/**
\page ToFKinectV2Page Microsoft Kinect V2

MITK-IGT accesses the Microsoft Kinect V2 (Kinect for Xbox One / Kinect for Windows v2) through
<a href="https://github.com/OpenKinect/libfreenect2">libfreenect2</a> on all platforms.
The Microsoft Kinect for Windows SDK 2.0 is not used.

\section ToFKinectV2PageBuildLibfreenect2 Building libfreenect2

Build libfreenect2 from source and install it. libusb and libjpeg-turbo are required; on Linux,
libfreenect2 packages of your distribution (e.g. from the AUR) can be used instead if they are
built with the options below.

\code
cmake -S libfreenect2 -B libfreenect2-build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=<libfreenect2-install> -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 -DENABLE_CXX11=ON -DENABLE_CUDA=OFF -DENABLE_OPENCL=OFF -DENABLE_VAAPI=OFF -DBUILD_OPENNI2_DRIVER=OFF
cmake --build libfreenect2-build --config Release --target install
\endcode

MITK processes the depth data with the CPU pipeline of libfreenect2, so OpenCL and CUDA are not
needed (the OpenCL code of libfreenect2 does not compile against current OpenCL headers).
VA-API must be disabled because the VA-API JPEG decoder crashes with some graphics drivers;
libjpeg-turbo decodes the color images instead.

On Windows, the libfreenect2 finders do not always match the current libusb and libjpeg-turbo
binary packages. If they are not found, pass <tt>LibUSB_INCLUDE_DIRS</tt>, <tt>LibUSB_LIBRARIES</tt>,
<tt>TurboJPEG_INCLUDE_DIRS</tt> and <tt>TurboJPEG_LIBRARIES</tt> explicitly.

\section ToFKinectV2PageBuildMITK Enabling the Kinect V2 in MITK

Configure the inner MITK build (<tt>MITK-superbuild/MITK-build</tt>) with:

\code
-DMITK_USE_TOF_KINECTV2=ON
-DMITK_BUILD_org.mitk.gui.qt.tofutil=ON
-Dfreenect2_DIR=<libfreenect2-install>/lib/cmake/freenect2
\endcode

On Windows, <tt>&lt;libfreenect2-install&gt;/bin</tt> (containing <tt>freenect2.dll</tt>,
<tt>libusb-1.0.dll</tt> and <tt>turbojpeg.dll</tt>) is added to the library search path automatically,
so the generated start scripts and the tests find these DLLs.

\section ToFKinectV2PageDriver Installing the USB driver

libfreenect2 talks to the camera through libusb, which requires a generic USB driver instead of the
Microsoft Kinect driver.

<b>Windows:</b> Without a suitable driver, the camera interface of the Kinect appears as
"Xbox NUI Sensor" with an error in the Device Manager, and connecting in MITK fails with
"No Kinect 2 found!". Install WinUSB with <a href="https://zadig.akeo.ie">Zadig</a>:

<ol>
  <li>Connect the Kinect V2 and close MITK.</li>
  <li>In Zadig, select <i>Options - List All Devices</i>.</li>
  <li>Select <b>Xbox NUI Sensor (Interface 0)</b>. Leave the other interfaces (audio) unchanged.</li>
  <li>Select <b>WinUSB</b> as target driver and click <i>Replace Driver</i>.</li>
</ol>

The camera can then no longer be used by applications based on the Microsoft Kinect SDK. To revert,
uninstall the device in the Device Manager including its driver and reconnect the Kinect.

<b>Linux:</b> Install the udev rules shipped with libfreenect2
(<tt>platform/linux/udev/90-kinect2.rules</tt>) to <tt>/etc/udev/rules.d/</tt> to access the camera
without root privileges, then reconnect the Kinect.

The <tt>Protonect</tt> example installed with libfreenect2 can be used to check the camera
independently of MITK.

\section ToFKinectV2PageUsage Usage

Open the <i>ToF Util</i> view, select <b>Kinect V2</b> in the device list and click <i>Connect</i>.
MITK uses the default intrinsics from <tt>KinectV2_IR_camera.xml</tt> shipped with the module, not a
per-device calibration. The acquisition mode (RGB or IR) can only be changed while the camera is
disconnected.

*/
